00/K=10000 ratio < 2 vs Stein-CLT prediction 7.07); pure Gaussian decays as 1/√K (ratio 6.42 ≈ √50). Plateau magnitude W_1/σ tracks |kurtosis| monotonically across the 9 families. Linear interp at kurt=-0.41 (D_emp's kurtosis) predicts W_1/σ ≈ 0.042; observed W_1/σ = 0.038 — within 10%. Conclusion: the W_1 plateau is a generic kurtosis-driven W_1(P, N(μ_P, σ_P²)) measurement, fully predicted by D's kurtosis under log-uniform x. The cross-domain Stein technique import survives but is not load-bearing for the conclusion. See `experiments/analytic/stein_wasserstein_pi/stein_wasserstein_pi_results.md`, `experiments/analytic/stein_wasserstein_pi/test_plateau_universality_results.md`, `novel/finite_x_wasserstein_plateau.md`, `archive/sessions/session112_verify_c5_stein.md`, `archive/sessions/session113_verify_c5_stein.md`. **S114 verify-6 PARTIAL:** S108's W_1=0.00829 reproduces to 0.3% under three independent W_1 implementations (mid-rank quantile, scipy MC Gaussian reference, CDF-integral); S108 number is solid. SEPARATELY, S113's "kurtosis fully predicts W_1/σ within 10%" claim REFUTED on Beta(α,α) at analytically-tuned α=5.817 to give kurt=-0.41 (matching D_emp): Beta gives W_1/σ = 0.0328 vs S113-predicted 0.0426 vs D_emp 0.0376 (gaps -23% / -13%). S113 universality is qualitative (any non-Gaussian distribution plateaus) but NOT quantitatively kurtosis-only. S108 grade stays B. **S115 verify-7 CONFIRM:** the sub-window correlation r=0.906 (S108 structural-matching argument, originally cited as evidence of low-zero structural origin) IS Riemann-φ-specific. 200-trial random-phase null preserving the same Riemann γ_k gives mean r = -0.04 ± 0.39 (matches n=10 noise floor 1/√n ≈ 0.316), with 0/200 trials reaching r ≥ 0.906 (one-sided p < 0.005, z=+2.44 above null mean). Random-phase + non-Riemann γ ∈ [10,145] and pure-noise controls give statistically identical distributions (means within 0.02, stds within 0.01). The actual zero phases are necessary AND sufficient to repro